[Music]  in an age long forgotten preceding the  dark chapters and high rules distant  past three golden goddesses created the  world granting it physical form  autonomous life forms and lost who  uphold after the divine task was done  these spectral beings left this plane of  existence leaving only a symbol of their  power behind in the form of the almighty  Triforce however upon their departure a  more sinister force was unleashed as  well a curse was cast upon the  unexpected inhabitants of this newly  created reality a wave of dark cypher  rippled through Hyrule investing any  living soul it could find seizing  control of the neurons of its hosts and  suppressing their reasoning memory and  advanced motor functions the result was  a population stuck in strange behavior  loops repeating the same lines in  cohesive speech patterns and loss of the  ability to perform advanced motion  standing frozen in place or cursed at  walk the same path over and over for no  damn reason  additionally time bubbles were created  around the big population centers across  the land preventing time from flowing  naturally unless unobserved turning  quantum theory on its head or you know  it might have had something to do with  the limitations of primitive hardware  [Music]  MPC's or non-player characters have  historically been rather crucial in the  Zelda series handing out important hints  items or advice however in terms of  their AI if you can even call it that  there wasn't exactly anything special  about them with most of them glued to  the ground and endlessly repeating the  same animation in lines in that regard  the early NPCs basically functioned as  glorified signposts with the passing of  each new iteration in the Zelda series  we've slowly seen NPCs acting more and  more like actual people Majora's Mask  with its three day time cycle allowed  for richer character development with  inhabitants having actual schedules and  daily routines and I'd argued that to  this day Clock Town is still one of the  most believable fictional towns ever  constructed the Wind Waker and Twilight  Princess saw NPCs walking around going  on daily strolls indulging in gossip or  going on like a thieving missions  however one thing was missing from these  games the inhabitants of Hyrule or  termina rarely left the safety of their  respective towns or cities characters  talked about it telling stories of their  travels or days out at sea but we almost  never got to actually see it happen with  our own eyes in the Wind Waker we barely  saw any friendly ships out on the open  waters or sailing from island to island  in general it was quite rare to meet a  fellow adventurer out on the road making  the world feel less realistic and  connected how do these shops keep up  their supplies how do people travel  between towns and how the hell is this  guy still in business breath of the wild  made a radical change in this department  despite the world being enormous in  decline and more dangerous than it's  ever been before we can meet many  different characters out on the road  people risking their lives to gather  resources merchants travelling between  towns travellers and adventurers going  in and out of stables treasure hunters  looking for fortunes archeologists and  researchers studying different aspects  of Hyrule distant past now for link this  is of course nothing special as he is  missed a hero man savior of worlds but  it's refreshing to see other inhabitants  traversing the landscape it all  contributes to making Hyrule despite its  broken stay  feel like a world being lived in which  brings us to the main subject of the  video throughout Hyrule we can find  plenty of NPCs who have chosen to leave  the safety of their village life behind  to set up camp out there in the wild  each with their own personal reasons  whether it's running a trading business  a spiritual quest or just a general  thirst for adventure some set up camp in  very remote locations others are idiots  and camp right in the path of traveling  becau blends and how kidding this guy  gets his ass kicked on a daily basis  tests move your camp dude however more  interestingly sometimes you will stumble  upon an abandoned campsite with its  occupant no longer around some of these  can be found in rather interesting areas  just the fact that you can reach a  desolate and remote location and find  out someone's already been there has a  feeling of a mystique around it it makes  you curious about who this person is or  was what happened to them and where are  they now  are they even still alive in some cases  only the remnants of a campfire remain  yet sometimes we can find other  materials as well and even a tent still  completely intact so let's go over some  of the most interesting spots found  across Hyrule the many bridges found  around Hyrule don't have anything  interesting underneath not even the  great bridge of hylia the only one that  does have something noteworthy is  Kakariko bridge there is a small  abandoned campsite with a cooking pot a  roasted bass some boxes and food  leftovers and a chest containing ice  arrows now it's not so much the campsite  itself that's interesting but rather the  location now I don't know if this is  what they were going for but I'd like to  think that this is a reference to the  man under the bridge in a Link to the  Past if you visit him by swimming under  the bridge he will grant you one of four  empty bottles you can obtain in the game  so most veterans are familiar with this  guy the fact that none of the other  bridges have anything of interest  underneath them and this campsite is  just randomly here feels like a nod to  this particular location whoever was  camping here previously they are no  longer around to hand out bottles to  strangers  if you've seen my video about the low  male labyrinths you may remember me  mentioning that there's a camp site in  the eastern section of the maze near the  outer wall this location has intrigued  me since who other than link would be  daring enough to venture into such a  large complex and dangerous structure  all by themselves well I think I found  the answer to that question to the  southeast not too far from the labyrinth  at the snow field stable we find an old  man by the name of mountain he shares a  childhood story of two of his friends  Harry and Mary who both disappeared and  were never heard from again  Mary allegedly slipped off a cliff while  they were looking for the entrance to  the Forgotten temple and in all  probability didn't survive the long fall  down into Tanager Canyon the tale of  Harry is where it gets interesting in  his younger years Moncton and Harry went  out to play in the maze ignoring the  warning of the adults who said not to go  near it as it was too dangerous one day  Moncton urged Harry to travel deeper  into the maze as a test of courage after  which he was never seen or heard from  again  so could this campsite belong to Harry  who after being lost inside the  confusing and complex corridors of this  massive structure had to rest and regain  his strength ah but see there's one  piece of information I've left out of  the story at the end of the story about  Mary's unfortunate fate Moncton reveals  the real truth and states that although  both were never seen or heard from again  two of their descendants are in the  stall right outside that's right Harry  and Mary weren't actually people and  Moncton reveals that his only friends  growing up in this remote location were  horses so with this information we can  make a strong assumption that whoever  was camping inside the maze was in all  probability making a desperate attempt  to look for Harry this could have been  Moncton himself although he does mention  that he was just a kid when the incident  occurred so either he was one badass kid  or it was one of the stable owners most  likely his father who went out to look  for that precious horse while inside the  maze he may have gotten lost himself and  was forced to halt his search and spend  the night within the walls of the  freezing north low male average  harry was never seen again and probably  either died from hypothermia starvation  or fell prey to the red lizalfos who  made their home inside these walls one  interesting detail that may or may not  have a connection to this tale at night  around the outer walls of the maze there  will always be Estelle cobbling riding  an undead stell horse  are these the resurrected remains of  Harry  I found another abandoned campsite very  similar to the one inside the North Loma  labyrinth with the same makeshift flag  campfire and this decayed piece of cloth  in my video about the labyrinth I refer  to this as the possible remains of a  tent but on closer inspection this  doesn't seem to be the case it's  basically a mattress made from pieces of  cloth and some straw other survivalists  out there are using the same method I'm  not 100% sure what the flag is for but  my best guess would be that it serves as  either a beacon or functions as a  weather vane to detect bad weather some  of the other Hylian travelers and even  some stables also have one of these  flags set up which leads me to believe  that this person was most likely a  Hylian unlike some of the other  abandoned campsites where the tent is  still present this person may have  survived this temporary hold up and  packed up their tent and moved on  whoever this person is or was they  definitely have a massive set of balls  on them as the location of this camp  site isn't exactly conventional it's  next to Hyrule Castle right smack in the  middle of the castle town ruins the  castle town watchtower to be exact I  mean this place is crawling with  Guardian stalkers and there's malice  everywhere this campsite was undoubtedly  set up after the great calamity since it  wouldn't make sense to build a camp  inside of a watchtower which is still  intact  so who is this mystery camper well the  answer can be found south of hyrule  castle at the wetland stable I think  this daredevil is none other than this  woman named Parsi she's actually tied to  a side quest called the Royal Guards  gear where you have to show her a piece  of equipment from the Royal Guard series  either a bow a shield sword claymore or  halberd anything will do  Parsi admits that she herself used to  sneak into hyrule castle to look for  this equipment but never managed to find  any she also states that hyrule castle  used to be a lot less dangerous than it  is now  zelda seal on calamity Ganon slowly  weaken the castle became more and more  of a risk to visit as a matter of fact  it's pretty much confirmed that people  used to go into the ruins of Hyrule  Castle to investigate more back in the  day as rumor mill vol 7 tells the story  of King Rome's secret room and Zelda's  laboratory also known as Princess Zelda  study  something no-one but the royal family  insiders could have known about  nonetheless getting into hyrule castle  all by yourself to look for some Royal  Guards gear spending the night inside  the ruins of castle town  this woman has my respect a true display  of bravery hearing a story about  something is one thing but actually  finding proof like this makes the world  all the more believable and it goes to  show how much little details the zelda  team have hidden around Hyrule there are  many more examples of abandoned camping  spots with little stories attached to  them like hearing someone in Llewelyn  say that monsters have been taking up  the best fishing spots only to find an  empty campsite on the shoreline on the  other side of the mountains with pikarin  scouring through the remains discovering  a tent with a set of snow walls near a  secret stone door into the mountains  indicating that someone else already  tried to solve this mystery but was  interrupted and had to leave their  venture behind hearing the stories of a  lone adventurer who tried to reach the  northern edge of Habra but was forced to  turn back because of bad weather and  strong winds ho because you wanted to  know what was there or how about the  mystery visitor on the equally  mysterious hard-to-reach makar island  who left behind nothing but a campfire  under a dead tree link isn't the only  one out there looking for answers a big  shout-out goes to Tim Demong an amazing  musician and fellow dutchy who provided  the Clock Town theme you heard that the  beginning of the video there's much more  where that came from so please go over  to his channel and show this man some  love the link is in the description and  of course a massive thanks to each and  every one of you for 10,000 subscribers  that is bananas I'll hold on bananas if  anyone would have told me one month ago  I would reach this number by June I  would have called them crazy this  milestone came a bit too fast to include  something special in this video but I  can still see if I can do something  special for next week if you have any  suggestions about what you want to see  that can steer my decision feel free to  share them down in the comments and that  is all for now next week we're gonna go  dungeon crawling again so I hope you  will join me on this adventure have a  great rest of your weekend  this is dawn signing off and have a good  one  you

